{"name":"Windsurf Blue","hex":"#718bae","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"windsurf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To ride a surfboard that has an attached sail"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of windsurfing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/windsurf"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Windsurfing","blocks":[{"text":"Windsurfing is a wind-propelled water sport that is a combination of sailing and surfing. It is also referred to as \"sailboarding\" and \"boardsailing\", and emerged in the late 1960s from the Californian aerospace and surf culture. Windsurfing gained a popular following across Europe and North America by the late 1970s and had achieved significant global popularity by the 1980s. Windsurfing became an Olympic sport in 1984."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Hood_river_windsurfers_20060701_0759.jpeg/330px-Hood_river_windsurfers_20060701_0759.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsurfing"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif/250px-Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif/250px-Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Darby sailboard, Popular Science, 1965","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/500px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/1280px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/500px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drake windsurfer, patent drawing, 1967","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindsurfpatent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https